const scienceChecks = `SCIENCE CHECKS for both student text AND visualBrief/activity.image.scene:
- Deformation means a change in SHAPE, not a change in speed or motion alone. Elastic deformation recovers when a modest load is removed; plastic deformation can remain. Clay and playdough do NOT spontaneously regain a ball shape after release. Prefer a soft rubber ball or sponge for temporary recovery examples.
- Distinguish an object's outer shape from its unseen internal structure. A still schematic cannot establish microscopic particle spacing or measured force. Do not invent internal cutaways as observations. If comparing outer shapes, do not assert the materials' microscopic mechanisms are identical.
- Do not rank a generic rubber ball and sponge by recovery speed, or claim that being "more elastic" necessarily makes an object recover faster. Such behavior depends on the particular material and loading. A useful boundary is simply that this comparison concerns visible shape changes, not identical internal structures or recovery behavior.
- Ordinary soft rubber balls AND elastic kitchen sponges can recover after release. Do not invent a contrast in which a generic sponge needs help to recover but a ball does not. If material-specific evidence is absent, limit the comparison to visible deformation under contact; it does not establish either object's recovery speed or internal mechanism.
- Coasting on a horizontal frictionless track does not require a continuing forward push, but gravity and a support force are still present. Say no net horizontal force, not no forces. A real sliding object slows because of interactions; inertia is not energy and is not a slowing force.
- Keep the SAME conditions throughout each activity: a frictionless, air-resistance-free horizontal cart cannot gradually stop. For a slowing cart/book, explicitly use a real surface with friction and air resistance from the start, not a frictionless track. Do not add an unused launcher to an everyday sliding-book scene.
- Energy can be stored/transferred; do not treat energy as a literal object whose physical spacing changes. Keep a counter/tray analogy to amounts and accounting. Add a boundary only when it prevents a likely misunderstanding without creating confusion.
- Verify every panel's before/during/after states against the named material. Text and image must agree; never show plastic clay recovering just because a neighboring elastic ball recovers.`;

export const MATERIAL_VISUAL_CHECKS = `VISUAL CLARITY AND CONSISTENCY:
- Make the relevant change visible in the object's silhouette, spacing, position or contact, not just in the caption. When comparing deformation, changing only the object's height above the floor does NOT show a change of shape.
- A compressed elastic ball has one smooth continuous outline with a broad contact region, not an extra foot, skirt, puddle or flared rim at its base. Enlarge the relevant shape change for clarity without turning solid deformation into melting.
- For a compressed soft ball, depict a clearly wider, lower oval with a broad flat contact patch, contrasted with the round unloaded ball; not two near-circles. Keep the same material, color, viewpoint and approximate volume, rather than resizing the whole object. For a pressed sponge, show a visible indentation under the touching hand: the front top edge visibly dips at the pressed center, with higher shoulders on both sides. Keep this changed outline visible below the fingers, not hidden behind the hand; do not draw an unchanged rectangular block with a hand merely resting on it. Keep contact on the surface, never sinking through the floor.
- These are schematics, not measurements. A small change may be visually emphasized for legibility; say in the relevant student stage that the shape change is emphasized for clarity. Do not invent a measured deformation amount.
- Choose ONE pictured action. Title, relevant stage text, panel description, caption and alt text must agree about the object, action and contact. A hand pressing a ball is not a ball falling onto the floor. If the scene is a bounce, omit hands; if it is hand pressure, explicitly describe that in its caption and matching stage. Alternative experiences can stay in text but must not leak into the pictured action.
- Preserve the equipment across time panels: an object may move, change shape or lose contact, but must not disappear or become a different component. For a spring launcher, keep its visible coil attached to the same fixed support in both panels; after release the coil is longer, with a clear gap before the cart. Do not replace the coil with a short peg. Apply this example only when a spring launcher is in the panel plan.
- Put every visually tested feature in the authoritative panel description, not only in scene/visualBrief. If contact deformation is the focus, keep the changed outline visible around the fingers. If an image only cues a remembered experience, do not claim that it proves a hidden dent, a change in speed or an unseen event.
- Name sustained pressure as pressing or compression, not a collision. Use collision/impact only when the setup describes objects moving into contact; a frozen view of that collision is still valid. If a ball is simply under a pressing plate, adjust a mismatched collision title to describe pressing, without inventing a new event or changing the lesson activity.`;

export const MATERIAL_INQUIRY_CHECKS = `STUDENT THINKING AND EVIDENCE:
- State one central thinking task per stage; a short reason or concrete comparison may support it. Do not stack several generic questions or provide a complete answer followed only by "What do you notice?".
- Analogy: make clear what is difficult to see or understand about the TARGET without inventing microscopic observations. At mapping, give a useful foothold and ask the learner to identify, compare or use a correspondence; do not turn the whole stage into a completed explanation. When limits is present and helps understanding, name one fair boundary and invite the learner to consider what the comparison cannot establish. Omit or rewrite a confusing limits stage; correct a misleading comparison itself instead of relying on a disclaimer. The analogue stage AND its caption/alt stay independent: tray/counter captions must not mention a launch, energy or the target before mapping.
- Experience bridging: notice revisits a recalled or imagined experience; do not unexpectedly require a ball, sponge, book or live experiment. A supplied picture may support recall, but separate what is visible now from what the learner remembers over time. After observation, name the concept briefly, then connect that name to what the learner noticed rather than explaining the whole mechanism.
- Cognitive conflict: distinguish illustrated shape/contact from explicitly hypothetical data. A still before/after image cannot establish speed or equal time intervals. If using positions at stated times, keep them in discrepant text, mark them as model values, and carry that source into observedOutcome. Do not present the model values as measured experimental evidence.
- Check image availability at each stage: conflict has no image before stage 2; analogy has its target panel at stage 0 and analogue panel at stage 1; experience bridging shows its image at revealAt (0 or 1). Do not tell a learner to inspect an image before it is available. Captions and alt text must not disclose a later mapping, result or concept name.
- End with a learner-written question about the selected target or lived experience. Offer question starters, not a finished question to copy. Keep reasonable simplifications and optional stages; do not add complexity just to make a draft seem more rigorous.`;

const analogyStudentChecks = `ANALOGY STUDENT FOLLOWABILITY (apply only to analogy):
- Make the target concept and the purpose of investigating it visible in the FIRST student stage, using a neutral goal in plain language. Naming what we will investigate is allowed; giving the scientific answer is not. Keep the same target throughout the activity.
- Choose a familiar analogue whose selected parts or relationship help with that target. Similar appearance alone is not a reason to compare. If the analogue's action represents a different relationship, revise the analogue or the mapping; a disclaimer cannot fix it.
- In the mapping stage's student text, name the exact target feature and analogue feature to compare. Give a small, accurate foothold, then leave one meaningful correspondence or implication for the learner to work out. The learner cannot see analogyMapping planning data, so do not leave essential guidance only there.
- Connect one concrete learner action to one tentative inference: identify or compare an available feature, then use that clue to think about the named target. A brief incomplete starter such as "These parts correspond because ..." can help students write or sketch a response; do not supply the completed response. At entities depth this can be a useful part-to-part match, without a hidden-mechanism explanation.
- Use only evidence available in the two static panels or the stated scenario. Do not ask students to observe a change, measurement or third view that is not provided. Any changed-condition question must say it is imagined and keep other conditions fixed, not claim a second experiment was shown.
- Limits is optional. Include one only when it clearly prevents a likely misunderstanding; omit or rewrite it if it introduces jargon, a new confusing mechanism or an irrelevant difference. Do not add a fifth stage just to fill space. End by asking the learner to write their own scientific question about the same unresolved target, using a general opener rather than a finished question.`;

